#15 positive.ts:45:5-47.4 - swallowed-exception: broad exception handler that ignores the error (empty, pass/continue, or only logs).
#16 positive.ts:50:4-20 - suppressed-diagnostic: diagnostic suppressed without a justification.
#17 positive.ts:53:1-61.2 - excessive-comments: function body is dominated by comments (6 of 9 non-blank lines are comments).
#18 positive.ts:68:11-26 - deep-nesting: control flow nested more than 4 levels deep (depth 5).
#19 positive.ts:80:1-165.2 - long-function: function has more than 80 lines of code (86 lines of code).
#20 positive.ts:179:67-189.2 - duplicated-block: block of code duplicates another block (first occurrence at positive.ts:167:63, lines 167-177).
#21 positive.ts:192:17-34 - ts-missing-optional-chain-expression: prefer using an optional chain expression instead, as it's more concise and easier to read (`prev && prev.name` -> `prev?.name`).
#22 positive.ts:193:17-60 - ts-missing-optional-chain-expression: prefer using an optional chain expression instead, as it's more concise and easier to read (`list && list[key] && list[key].label.trim()` -> `list?.[key]?.label.trim()`).
#23 positive.ts:197:36-39 - ts-explicit-any: specify a different type instead of `any`, as `any` turns off type checking for every value it reaches.
#24 positive.ts:197:81-84 - ts-explicit-any: specify a different type instead of `any`, as `any` turns off type checking for every value it reaches.
#25 positive.ts:198:3-12 - ts-var-declaration: use `let` or `const` instead of `var`, as block-scoped declarations avoid hoisting surprises (`var count` -> `let count`).
#26 positive.ts:199:7-27 - ts-loose-equality: use `===` and `!==` instead of `==` and `!=`, as loose equality converts the operands in surprising ways (`count == rows.length` -> `count === rows.length`).
#27 positive.ts:200:5-14 - ts-debugger-statement: remove this `debugger` statement, as it pauses the program whenever developer tools are open.
#28 positive.ts:202:16-48 - ts-prefer-array-find: prefer `.find()` over taking the first element of `.filter()`, as it stops at the first match and states the intent (`rows.filter((r) => r.ok).shift()` -> `rows.find((r) => r.ok)`).
#29 positive.ts:203:16-46 - ts-prefer-array-find: prefer `.find()` over taking the first element of `.filter()`, as it stops at the first match and states the intent (`rows.filter((r) => r.ok).at(0)` -> `rows.find((r) => r.ok)`).
#30 positive.ts:204:16-53 - ts-prefer-array-some: prefer `.some()` over comparing the length of `.filter()`, as it stops at the first match and returns a boolean (`rows.filter((r) => r.ok).length !== 0` -> `rows.some((r) => r.ok)`).
#31 positive.ts:205:19-41 - ts-prefer-includes: prefer `.includes()` over comparing the result of `.indexOf()`, as it states the intent directly (`words.indexOf("x") < 0` -> `!words.includes("x")`).
#32 positive.ts:206:16-39 - ts-prefer-at: prefer `.at()` with a negative index over subtracting from `.length`, as it avoids repeating the receiver (`words[words.length - 2]` -> `words.at(-2)`).
#33 positive.ts:207:15-49 - ts-prefer-object-has-own: prefer `Object.hasOwn()` over calling `hasOwnProperty` through the prototype, as it is shorter and easier to read (`{}.hasOwnProperty.call(user, "id")` -> `Object.hasOwn(user, "id")`).
#34 positive.ts:208:17-49 - ts-typeof-undefined: compare with `undefined` directly instead of using `typeof`, as it is shorter and easier to read (`typeof user.name !== "undefined"` -> `user.name !== undefined`).
#35 positive.ts:209:31-52 - ts-prefer-string-replace-all: prefer `.replaceAll()` over `.replace()` with a global regex, as it states the intent (`.replace(/\s+/g, " ")` -> `.replaceAll(/\s+/g, " ")`).
#36 positive.ts:210:15-38 - ts-number-static-methods: prefer the static methods of `Number` over the global functions, as they keep number helpers in one namespace (`isNaN(user.size as any)` -> `Number.isNaN(user.size as any)`).
